## Go server development

This module (`{{MODULE_PATH}}`) uses Clean Architecture. Apply these rules to
every feature, including ordinary requests such as "add a user API" that do not
explicitly mention architecture. If available, use `add-go-feature` for feature
implementation; the rules below also work without the plugin installed.
Use `go-clean-arch` when available for code review or structural refactoring.
A review request is read-only; refactoring preserves behavior, API contracts,
and the established stack unless the user requests those changes.

### Package responsibilities

- `internal/domain/`: business entities, values, invariants, and errors. Keep
  HTTP, ORM, SQL driver, JWT implementation, and environment concerns outside.
- `internal/usecase/<feature>/`: business decisions and orchestration. Declare
  small repository/external-service interfaces here, owned by their consumer.
- `internal/repository/<store>/`: persistence adapters and row/domain mapping.
  Implement the use-case ports; do not expose driver types or errors inward.
- `internal/transport/http/`: request/response DTOs, input parsing, caller
  identity, and status/error mapping. Inject use cases into handlers.
- `cmd/api/main.go`: configuration, resource lifecycle, concrete constructors,
  and route wiring. Keep business decisions out of this composition root.

Compile-time imports point inward: transport -> use case -> domain; repository
adapters -> domain and consumer ports; main -> the assembled layers. Use cases
must not import transport, repository implementations, or main. Domain must not
import use cases or adapters. Preserve this rule for any additional packages.

### Adding a feature

1. Read the closest existing feature, its routes, ports, and tests. Preserve
   established API contracts and the chosen framework/storage unless the task
   explicitly changes them.
2. Define the operation's inputs, outputs, errors, and business permissions.
   Implement domain rules and a use case before wiring the HTTP endpoint.
3. Add only the interfaces and adapters the operation consumes. Keep transactions
   atomic without passing `*sql.Tx`, `*gorm.DB`, or HTTP context through ports.
4. Implement HTTP mapping and inject concrete dependencies in the composition
   root. Propagate `context.Context`; return expected failures as errors.
5. Test business behavior and HTTP contracts, plus storage semantics when they
   change. Inspect imports to catch outward dependencies before completing work.

The initial `/health` endpoint is process liveness, so it has no artificial
business use case or database repository. Add real domain/use-case/repository
files when the first feature needs them; remove its directory's `.gitkeep` then.
Do not implement business operations directly in the router just because health
is handled there. Do not add a database, auth system, or chat feature by default.

### Verification

Format changed Go files, then run from this module:

```bash
go build ./...
go test ./...
go vet ./...
go list -f '{{.ImportPath}} -> {{join .Imports " "}}' ./...
```

Review the import listing against the boundaries above; it is not an automatic
pass/fail check. Run `go test -race ./...` when shared state or goroutines change.
Use isolated test infrastructure for integration checks. Preserve schema and
stored data during structural refactors, and keep migrations separate from
server startup. Report skipped checks and pre-existing failures precisely.
